A||||13||rogergarrison||Towing/ Trailering a Fiero||||1||
Z||000000||rogergarrison||05-16-2001||03:13 PM||||Im buying a two wheel, steering, tow dolly. I make a few trips like to Mid Ohio Race Track and like to take my car, but need the van to campout and carry stuff. Are there any issues with towing a 4 speed stick Fiero with rear wheels on the ground and front up on dolly? How about distance limits on towing. I may have to tow to Carlisle so I have my van to bring stuff back home. I didnt want to spend the money on a tandem trailer id only use a few times a year.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||04:49 PM||||you can tow a standard with the wheels onthe ground all you want. Its the autos that need to be towed with the rear wheels off the ground.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||05-16-2001||05:13 PM||||I prefer to tow all Fieros with the rear wheels on the dolly.  Most of the weight is in the rear and it makes it easier to tow (my opinion).  If you must tow it with the rears on the ground you should be OK as long as it is a manual.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroHarry||05-16-2001||06:16 PM||||With an automatic tranny the engine drives the transmission fluid pump (which in turn cools/lubes the transmission) towing forces the tranny gears to start turning without lubrication.<BR>If towing automatics with the rears on the road i always remove drive shafts.<BR>Note: Very hard to due with a Fiero huh?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B>Harry Waggoner</B><BR>1985 GT 263K+ <I>"TEMPTATION"</I><BR><B>"Im not driving to fast, Im flying to low!"</B>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierodave2001||05-17-2001||02:24 AM||||You could rent a trailer that puts all wheels off ground also.<P>That's what I'm doing,  Just have to get the weight right.||||reg||6
Z||000005||rogergarrison||05-17-2001||04:42 AM||||Thanks for info guys. Ive towed stuff for years from boats to house trailers. I was just looking for any info specific for Fieros. I know lots of you tow them. I know Honda says never tow theres on 2 wheel dollies. I know about the driveshaft thing with automatics, mines a standard. Driveshaft on a Fiero? What engine option is that in, LOL. I dont want to rent, prefer to own it so I can use it whenever I want.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||11:07 AM||||You should be able to pull it around just fine with a towbar too, can get one used for maybe $100?<P>  Unless the front end is messed up I dont think the two wheel dollys really add much value over a towbar for a mannytranny car being towed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||05-17-2001||12:41 PM||||I only paid $300 for the tow dolly. I thought about towbar, but i dont want to weld stuff under the front to hook it up. My kits too low in front. The dolly I bought, I just drive up on it, clamp the front wheels down and go.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wolfhound||05-17-2001||01:24 PM||||Roger, You said it was a steerable dolly. The dollys I've used have a beam that the front wheels sit on that pivots over a fixed axle. do you mean that the wheel positions are fixed and the tongue steers the dolly wheels? Sounds interesting, and lighter construction.||||reg||1
Z||000009||northstar87||05-17-2001||02:04 PM||||I believe the 4-speed transaxle is ok to tow with all 4 wheels on the ground.  It is lubed through gear splash from the carrier differential assembly.  With the drive wheels still on the ground it will continue to spin the diff and still have gear splash.  Should be fine.<P>Chris Moore<BR>ASE Master Auto Tech <BR>Auto Instructor<BR>1986 GT Northstar Convertible<BR>1987 GT Northstar<BR>1988 GT T-TOP 3.4 5 speed<BR>1988 GT T-TOP stock||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroHarry||05-17-2001||02:09 PM||||Alright Roger, you know I was kidding!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>So are you coming saturday or what?<P>------------------<BR><B>Harry Waggoner</B><BR>1985 GT 263K+ <I>"TEMPTATION"</I><BR><B>"Im not driving to fast, Im flying to low!"</B>||||reg||1
Z||000011||wkayl||05-17-2001||02:49 PM||||Just remember if you tow it with the rear wheels on the ground you will be adding miles to your car.  All drive line components up to and including parts of the transmission will be getting wear.  If you tow it backwards, the only thing getting wear are the front tires and wheel bearings (and a some suspension components).<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/twocars.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||05-17-2001||10:44 PM||||the dolly is rigid and the wheels steer on kingpins. has a hydraulic dampener on tie rod. My cars too wide in back, but id rather tow with front up. Ill tried to see how it fit today. have to do some custom work on the dolly so my spoiler wont hit. The tie down rachets are too high. We already figured out how were going to modify it, just have to wait a week or two.||||reg||1
